  "text": "The experiment compared two stimulant tablets—a low‑dose (15 mg) and a higher‑dose (60 mg)—with a placebo pill. Researchers recorded systolic blood pressure, heart rate, performance on a visual tracking task, and participants’ self‑ratings on several 9‑point scales covering boredom‑interest, relaxation‑strain, irritation‑pleasure, energy‑fatigue, sleepiness‑alertness, and attention‑inattention. The low‑dose stimulant slowed the improvement of tracking accuracy and reduced the likelihood of participants reporting boredom, irritation, fatigue, sleepiness, and inattention. It also caused an increase in systolic pressure and heart rate. The higher‑dose stimulant produced a comparable but weaker and shorter‑lasting effect on tracking performance and subjective ratings. It raised systolic pressure more noticeably than the low‑dose drug but had little effect on heart rate."
